# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# wrangler local dev cache (from testing Cloudflare headers/deploy locally)
.wrangler/

# claude code local settings
.claude/

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# scratch files from API probing
/live.json
/boot.json
/fix.json

# Third-party provider documentation kept locally for reference. The rule stands
# on size alone — the API-Football PDF is 7.3MB, and a git repo is a poor home for
# vendor binaries that are downloadable from the vendor. (It originally also said
# "not ours to redistribute from a public repo"; the repo is private now, which
# weakens that half of the argument but not the first.) docs/roadmap.md records
# what was actually read out of them, which is the part that needed to be durable.
/docs/*.pdf

# Working files for the promoted-clubs cold-start data drop (2026-08-05): raw
# FootyStats PDFs per player/club and a fabricated analytics CSV rejected as a
# data source (95% position archetypes, not real per-player data — see "A
# candidate data drop was rejected" in docs/roadmap.md). The verified
# premier_league_new_players.csv is tracked; everything else here is scratch
# or vendor material, not durable repo content.
/docs/Promoted Team Data/*.pdf
/docs/Promoted Team Data/premier_league_new_players_analytics.csv
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Coventry/
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Hull City/
/docs/Promoted Team Data/Ipswich/
